refined_question:
Let (x_n)_{n\ge 1} be a nonincreasing sequence of positive real numbers with \sum_{n=1}^\infty x_n < \infty, and for each n let r_n := \sum_{m>n} x_m. Assume that x_n < r_n for every n \in \mathbb{N}. Must there exist an infinite subsequence (x_{n_k})_{k\ge 1}, with n_1 < n_2 < \cdots, such that its achievement set
E(x_{n_k}) := \left\{\sum_{k=1}^\infty \varepsilon_k x_{n_k} : \varepsilon_k \in \{0,1\}\right\} \subset \mathbb{R}
is a Cantorval?
